PLACES=[
N('brownsville-carnegie-library-tn','Brownsville Carnegie Library (Chamber of Commerce)','Brownsville','tn','civic','standing',35.59403,-89.26365,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1913',
  "Brownsville got a $7,500 Carnegie grant on December 2, 1909. The one-storey Classical Revival library, built from 1910, is wood-framed with a brick skin over a full basement; the Register names W. J. Taylor, while the state list says it was possibly designed by Neander M. Woods Jr. of Memphis. Many sources say it opened in 1912, but its first annual report, found later, shows it opened on June 3, 1913. The Register nomination says it does not seem to have been segregated, which was unusual in the South. It was remodelled in 1957 and 1958. A new county library opened in 1992 (the state list says the old one closed in 2002), and after a state-funded renovation it reopened in 1994 as the Brownsville-Haywood County Chamber of Commerce. On the National Register since 2018.",
  'Brick painted yellow, with a grey parapet and CHAMBER OF COMMERCE over the door.',
  '','121 West Main Street, just west of the courthouse square. Chamber of Commerce offices: look from the street.',
  [W('Brownsville Carnegie Library'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/100002752'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/1ef9f609-b09e-4b3d-9818-05811d871aaa'],LK],
  brick='no',bcol='painted'),
N('etowah-carnegie-library-tn','Etowah Carnegie Library (and City Hall)','Etowah','tn','civic','standing',35.32423,-84.52611,'exact','open','walk','','1915–1916',
  "Built in 1915 and 1916 with an $8,000 Carnegie grant, a two-storey Colonial Revival building of brick, 80 feet wide, with granite lintels and a marble arch over the door carved CARNEGIE. It has been the town's only public library, its first high school from 1916 to 1922, and its city offices from 1916 to now. The library is upstairs, with its original built-in and freestanding wooden bookcases; the city hall is on the ground floor, in the room that was the high school's auditorium. On the National Register since 2003.",
  'CARNEGIE carved in the marble arch over the door, at the top of fourteen steps.',
  'The Register nomination shows an asphalt parking lot behind the building, on the east side.',
  '723–725 Ohio Avenue. The library upstairs and the city hall below. The library\'s own page says it is closed for renovation from November 21, 2026, until further notice.',
  [['Etowah Carnegie Library','https://sites.google.com/site/httpwwwetowahlibrary/home'],['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/03000132'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/9fca2bab-79d0-48e2-a3ab-e28d0298d915'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='Closed for renovation from November 21, 2026; usually Mon 10–3, Tue–Fri 10–8, Sat 10–3'),
N('greeneville-carnegie-library-tn','Greeneville Carnegie Library','Greeneville','tn','civic','standing',36.16230,-82.83177,'area','view','walk','','1914–1915',
  "Greeneville was granted $10,000 on November 3, 1913, and the library was built in 1914 and 1915 to plans by Aaron T. Simmons of Bloomington, Illinois. It was the town library until the 1970s, when the library moved to Main Street. It also held a women's rest room until 1975, and Waymarking calls it the first public rest room for women in Tennessee; the National Guard used it in the late 1970s. The state list, from a 2002 study, calls it vacant and says it was listed on the National Register in 1974, the year the Greeneville Historic District was listed. Its present use was not found.",
  '',
  '','109 West Summer Street. Look from the street; Waymarking says two dogwoods in front hide part of the building.',
  [['Waymarking: Carnegie Library, Greeneville','https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/WMBGY7_Carnegie_Library_Greeneville_TN'],LK],brick='?'),
N('harriman-carnegie-library-tn','Harriman Public Library (Carnegie building)','Harriman','tn','civic','standing',35.93371,-84.55118,'area','open','walk','','1909–1912',
  "A two-storey brick library on what was the town's public square, behind the old East Tennessee Land Company building that is now City Hall. Its front mixes Neo-Classical Revival, Beaux Arts and Colonial Revival: a pedimented portico with Doric columns, a cartouche, a fanlight over the door and arched windows, on a limestone base. The state list says it was built in 1909 and 1910 to plans by Paul O. Moratz of Bloomington, Illinois; the Register nomination gives 1912. Part of the Roane Street Commercial Historic District since 1989, and listed as still the public library.",
  'A pedimented portico on Doric columns.',
  '','601 Walden Avenue, behind City Hall. A public library; its hours were not found on its own page.',
  [['National Register nomination: Roane Street Commercial Historic District','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/0edd984b-2470-4ed9-969c-8363e10e9fa7'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('jackson-free-library-carnegie-tn','Carnegie Center for Arts and History (Jackson Free Library)','Jackson','tn','civic','standing',35.61610,-88.81687,'exact','open','walk','','1901–1903',
  "The Jackson Free Library, the town's first public library, paid for in part by a $30,000 Carnegie grant and opened in 1903; the cornerstone reads 1901. The state list names Anthony Ten Eyck Brown and Frederick H. Brown of Nashville as architects; the Register record says unknown. A two-storey Italian Renaissance front with a limestone doorway and iron gates, and a dome skylight. It served until a larger library opened in 1968. The state list says it was rehabilitated in 1981 as an art and history centre, Wikipedia that the city began restoring it in 1987. It is now the Carnegie Center for Arts and History, with the Legends of Tennessee Music museum and two galleries. On the National Register since 1975.",
  'Light buff brick mixed with a darker orange brick, limestone and a red tile roof.',
  '','305 East College Street, at Church Street. A museum: tickets $10, children 16 and under free, $5 seniors and military, free to Jackson-Madison County residents, sold only at the door.',
  [['The Carnegie: visit','https://www.jacksoncarnegie.com/visit'],W('Carnegie Center for Arts and History'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/75001769'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/84eed270-4971-4839-9710-8bffdd9325bd'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='Wed–Sat 10–4'),
N('mountain-branch-carnegie-library-johnson-city','Carnegie Library, Mountain Branch of the National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers','Johnson City','tn','civic','standing',36.31056,-82.37333,'area','view','walk','','1902–1904',
  "Congressman Walter P. Brownlow got money from Andrew Carnegie and others for a library at the Mountain Branch, a home for Union veterans whose Beaux Arts campus was designed by the New York architect Joseph H. Freelander and opened in 1903. The state list says the library was built from 1902 to 1904 with $16,000 and rehabilitated in 1998. It is Building 17, south of Memorial Hall, and the Park Service says it is one of only two known libraries at a National Home branch. The VA and the East Tennessee State University medical school use it as a lecture hall. The campus is a National Historic Landmark district, listed in 2011.",
  '',
  '','On the Mountain Home VA Medical Center campus, south of Memorial Hall. A lecture hall: look from outside. Whether visitors may walk the grounds was not found; the National Cemetery on the same campus has a visitors\' centre.',
  [['National Park Service: Mountain Branch','https://www.nps.gov/places/mountain-branch-mountain-home-tennessee.htm'],W('Mountain Branch, National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/11000560'],LK],brick='?'),
N('knoxville-free-colored-library-site','Site of the Free Colored Library (Carnegie branch)','Knoxville','tn','civic','gone',35.97040,-83.91100,'area','view','walk','','1916–1960s',
  "Knoxville's Carnegie branch for Black readers, at the corner of Nelson Avenue and East Vine Street. Carnegie gave $10,000 in 1916, and the library, designed by the local architect Albert B. Baumann Jr., opened in May 1918. BlackPast says it closed in 1961 and was later pulled down; the state list says it closed and was demolished in 1965. Nothing of it stands.",
  '',
  '','Near Nelson Avenue in east Knoxville. Nothing of the library stands.',
  [['BlackPast: Free Colored Library, Knoxville','https://www.blackpast.org/african-american-history/free-colored-library-knoxville-tennessee-1918-1961/'],LK],brick='?'),
N('nashville-carnegie-main-library-site','Site of the Carnegie Library of Nashville','Nashville','tn','civic','gone',36.16260,-86.78443,'area','view','walk','','1903–1963',
  "In 1901 Andrew Carnegie offered Nashville $100,000 for a library if the city gave $10,000 a year to run it. The cornerstone was laid on April 27, 1903, and the library opened on September 19, 1904; the library's own history gives its address as 225 Polk Avenue, the state list as 222 8th Avenue North. The state list names Albert Randolph Ross of New York as architect, with Thompson, Gibel and Asmus of Nashville, and says it was integrated between 1949 and 1954, closed in 1963 and was pulled down for the Ben West Library. What stands on the site now was not checked.",
  '',
  '','8th Avenue North in downtown Nashville. Nothing of the Carnegie library stands.',
  [['Nashville Public Library: library history','https://library.nashville.gov/about/library-history'],LK],brick='?'),
N('north-branch-library-nashville','North Branch Library (Carnegie)','Nashville','tn','civic','standing',36.17416,-86.79589,'exact','open','walk','','1913–1915',
  "One of two branches paid for by a 1912 Carnegie gift of $50,000, opened in 1915 at the southwest corner of Monroe Street and 10th Avenue, in the Beaux Arts style by C. K. Colley. It is the oldest library building Nashville still has, and one of only two of its Carnegie libraries left. Its floors, woodwork and checkout desk are original, and the auditorium downstairs keeps its 1915 stage. The state list says it was rehabilitated from 1981 to 1983; it is in the Buena Vista Historic District.",
  'The original 1915 stage in the auditorium downstairs.',
  'Free parking.',
  '1001 Monroe Street, at 10th Avenue North. A working public library.',
  [['Nashville Public Library: North Branch','https://library.nashville.gov/locations/north'],['Nashville Public Library: library history','https://library.nashville.gov/about/library-history'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='Mon–Fri 10–6, Sat 10–5, Sun closed'),
N('east-branch-library-nashville','East Branch Library (Carnegie)','Nashville','tn','civic','standing',36.17896,-86.75011,'exact','open','walk','','1917–1919',
  "Nashville's fourth Carnegie library, built with $25,000 from Carnegie and opened on May 8, 1919, at the junction of Main and 11th streets; the state list names Clarence K. Colley as architect. It has come through floods and tornadoes, and in 2000 a full restoration undid changes made in the 1960s. Limestone outside, with a marble foyer, large chandeliers and oak tables and chairs. A Metro Historic Landmark, and on the National Register since 1982.",
  'Limestone outside; a marble foyer and big chandeliers inside.',
  'Free parking.',
  'The library gives its address as 206 North 11th Street; the state list gives 206 Gallatin Avenue. A working public library.',
  [['Nashville Public Library: East Branch','https://library.nashville.gov/locations/east'],['Nashville Public Library: library history','https://library.nashville.gov/about/library-history'],LK],
  brick='no',bcol='none',hours='Mon, Wed, Fri 10–6; Tue, Thu 12–8; Sat 10–5; Sun closed'),
N('nashville-negro-branch-library-site','Site of the Negro Branch Library (Carnegie)','Nashville','tn','civic','gone',36.16017,-86.79012,'site','view','walk','','1916–about 1969',
  "Nashville's Carnegie branch for Black readers. The library's own history says it opened in 1916 at 12th and Hines Avenue and was sold in 1948, being no longer handy for its readers; the state list gives 220 12th Avenue North, built 1916 to 1918 to plans by Clarence K. Colley and Robert Sharp, closed in 1949 and pulled down about 1969. What stands on the lot now was not checked.",
  '',
  '','220 12th Avenue North. Nothing of the library stands.',
  [['Nashville Public Library: library history','https://library.nashville.gov/about/library-history'],LK],brick='?'),
N('fisk-carnegie-library-academic-building','Carnegie Library (Academic Building), Fisk University','Nashville','tn','inst','standing',36.16762,-86.80503,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1909',
  "Fisk's $20,000 Carnegie library, its cornerstone laid in 1908 by William Howard Taft, then Secretary of War. Wikipedia and the Register credit the design to the Black architect Moses McKissack III, his first big project; the state list says the design is attributed to Shepley, Rutan and Coolidge of Boston, with McKissack the builder. A two-storey Classical Revival building of brick with a stone porch on columns and a light well inside; the upper floor was meant for concerts. It is now Fisk's Academic Building. On the National Register on its own since 1985 (the state list gives 1977) and part of the Fisk University Historic District.",
  'A columned stone porch on a brick front.',
  '','Meharry Boulevard at 17th Avenue North, on the Fisk campus. A college building: look from outside.',
  [W('Fisk University Carnegie Library'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/85003769'],LK],brick='?'),
N('knoxville-college-carnegie-library-site','Site of the Knoxville College Carnegie Library','Knoxville','tn','inst','gone',35.96996,-83.94246,'area','view','walk','','1909–1966',
  "Knoxville College, a historically Black college, got $10,000 from Carnegie in 1907, and its library was built in 1909 to plans by Martin E. Parmelee of Knoxville. It was pulled down in 1966, and the Alumni Memorial Library was built in the same place in 1967. Wikipedia says most of the campus has stood abandoned since the late 1990s, with many fires in empty buildings, and that there are plans to renovate the Alumni Library.",
  '',
  '','The Knoxville College campus, 901 Knoxville College Drive. The campus is private and mostly abandoned: look only from the public street, and do not go into the buildings.',
  [W('Knoxville College'),LK],brick='?'),
N('lmu-carnegie-vincent-library-harrogate','Carnegie-Vincent Library, Lincoln Memorial University','Harrogate','tn','inst','standing',36.58011,-83.65947,'area','open','walk','','1904–1906',
  "Andrew Carnegie gave Lincoln Memorial University $20,000 in 1904 for a library, and it opened in 1906; the state list attributes the design to Gideon T. Spencer, the university's superintendent of buildings. Besides books on Lincoln, the Civil War and Appalachia, it held classrooms, a chapel and offices for a time. The Bert Vincent Memorial Wing was added in 1974, with more work in 1987, 1989 and 2008. Still the university library.",
  '',
  '','6965 Cumberland Gap Parkway, on the campus quad. A college library; its hours were not found on its own page.',
  [['Lincoln Memorial University: Carnegie-Vincent Library history','https://library.lmunet.edu/uasc/campus_histories/cvl'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('peabody-library-vanderbilt','Peabody Library, Vanderbilt University (Carnegie)','Nashville','tn','inst','standing',36.14246,-86.79920,'exact','view','walk','','1917–1919',
  "Carnegie committed $180,000 in 1917, which Vanderbilt's library calls the last and largest gift he made toward an academic library. The New York architect Edward L. Tilton designed it in red brick in the Beaux-Arts style, in keeping with the campus modelled on the University of Virginia, and about 45,000 books were moved in on May 30, 1919. Peabody College joined Vanderbilt in 1979, and this is now the oldest library on the Vanderbilt campus. Part of the Peabody College National Historic Landmark.",
  'Red brick in the Beaux-Arts style.',
  '','1210 21st Avenue South, on the Peabody campus. A university library; its own page limits entry at some hours to Vanderbilt students, staff and alumni. Look from outside.',
  [['Vanderbilt Libraries: Peabody Library, a brief history','https://betweenthelines.library.vanderbilt.edu/article/peabody-library-brief-history-given-at-centennial-celebration'],['Vanderbilt Libraries: Peabody Library hours','https://www.library.vanderbilt.edu/peabody/hours/'],LK],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',apart='peabody-college-campus-nashville: that entry is the whole Peabody campus; this is its Carnegie library building'),
N('ut-carnegie-library-austin-peay-knoxville','Austin Peay Building (old Carnegie Library), University of Tennessee','Knoxville','tn','inst','standing',35.95729,-83.92697,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1911',
  "Carnegie pledged $40,000 in 1905, and the $54,000 library, by Patton and Miller of Chicago, opened on May 30, 1911: brick with marble trim, two and a half storeys over a basement, with stacks for 75,000 books. A new library opened in 1931, and in 1934 and 1935 the old one was rebuilt and enlarged with Public Works Administration money, to plans by Charles Barber, as the Austin Peay administration building. The state list says it was taken down above its foundations; the university's own history says it was renovated and expanded. The administration left in 1974, and from 1975 it housed the psychology department.",
  '',
  '','1404 Circle Drive, on the University of Tennessee campus. A university building: look from outside.',
  [['University of Tennessee Volopedia: Carnegie Library','https://volopedia.lib.utk.edu/?p=2526'],['University of Tennessee Volopedia: Austin Peay Building','https://volopedia.lib.utk.edu/entries/austin-peay-building'],LK],brick='?'),
N('tusculum-carnegie-library-garland','Thomas J. Garland Library (Carnegie Hall), Tusculum University','Tusculum','tn','inst','standing',36.17406,-82.76084,'exact','open','walk','','1910–1911',
  "Tusculum and Washington College, merged for a time, won a $21,000 Carnegie pledge, and after a court case it was split equally between them. Tusculum's half built Carnegie Hall in 1910: two storeys of red brick on a cut limestone base, more a building for everything than a library, with a gymnasium, an oval running track on a balcony, classrooms and the library in the basement. After a new gym was built in 1927 it became all library. A stack wing came in 1961, it was renamed Tate Library in 1991, enlarged and reopened in 2005, and named the Thomas J. Garland Library in 2008. The college's library page says the building still has its indoor running track, which it calls the only one in an American library. Part of the Tusculum College Historic District since 1980.",
  'An indoor running track round the old gymnasium, now the reading room.',
  '','On the Tusculum University campus; Clio gives 60 Shiloh Road, the state list 910 Erwin Highway. A college library; its hours were not found on its own page.',
  [['Tusculum University library: history and renovation','https://tusculum.libguides.com/history_of_the_library/history_and_renovation'],['Clio: Thomas J. Garland Library','https://theclio.com/tour/828/3'],['National Register nomination: Tusculum College Historic District','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/279d6d92-bfa9-460b-8980-0f5b423e8fe9'],LK],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='?'),
N('washington-college-carnegie-temple-hall','Carnegie-Temple Hall, Washington College','Limestone','tn','inst','standing',36.22129,-82.57230,'area','view','walk','','1909–1910',
  "Washington College's half of the Carnegie gift it shared with Tusculum built Carnegie Hall about 1909: an administration building with a gymnasium and auditorium upstairs, also used as a library. In 1926 Mary Boyce Temple paid for two wings in memory of her father, Judge Oliver Perry Temple, and it was renamed Temple Hall. It faces the campus quadrangle with Harris Hall and Salem Presbyterian Church. The school closed in 2000; in 2002 the Register nomination said the administrative offices were still in this building, while the state list, from the same year, calls it vacant. Wikipedia says the school has since reopened as a local arts school. Part of the Washington College Historic District since 2002.",
  '',
  '','On the Washington College campus quadrangle, off Doak Lane. A private school: look from the road.',
  [W('Washington College Academy'),['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/02000812'],['National Register nomination','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/a10c0185-8c68-466e-b2be-86a7f532f536'],LK],brick='?'),
]
